<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  RUT_DroidRepairJob - DROID_REPAIR_FOR_PROFIT_EVENTS_1 (packet C5).

  Owner ruling 2, 2026-09-06 (DROID_UNIFIED_FRAMEWORK_DESIGN.md section 0):
  "there should be regular events where various local friendlies come to have you
  repair or upgrade their droids for profit... it's just what everyone knows Jawa
  are supposed to do. And there should be real opportunities to use superior or
  inferior parts, get rid of problematic droids or parts, and otherwise be...
  Jawa."

  THE SPEC IT WAS BUILT FROM (skills/rimworld-quests/SKILL.md section 2):
    ask       a neighbouring friendly walks a seized droid to your shop; get it
              moving again before their people come back for it
    reason    everyone on this rock knows Jawa fix droids. Turning it away is
              turning away the one thing your clan is known for.
    choice    WHICH part you fit. Droidworks already grades a fitted part by its
              CompQuality: an excellent part buys reputation and the best fee, a
              worn part buys a smaller fee and a worse name while you keep the
              good stock. Or you arrest the droid and it is simply yours.
    failure   never touched it (Fail, goodwill down), or the droid does not walk
              back out at all (Fail, goodwill well down) - the colony survives
              both
    reward    outcome-scaled silver by drop pod, plus goodwill
    deadline  5-9 days, shown as the quest timer; the OFFER expires in 2-4 days

  RESOLUTION MECHANISM IS B4b's, NOT A NEW ONE. The droid arrives as a quest
  lodger in the player's own faction (joinPlayer, the vanilla Hospitality shape),
  which is what makes ITab_Pawn_Health.ShouldAllowOperations() return true - a
  friendly NON-player-faction pawn cannot be operated on at all, verified in
  Assembly-CSharp. The player then runs the ALREADY-BUILT
  RSW_DW_Install{LegActuator,ManipulatorArm,Sensor,Motivator,Servo} recipes
  (Recipe_InstallDroidPartAtBench, which needs an RSW_DW_RepairBench within 15
  tiles) off the droid's health tab, consuming their OWN RSW_DW_Part_* stock.
  Nothing here duplicates that.

  ANTI-EXPONENTIAL GUARDRAIL (design doc section 6, "repair-for-profit events |
  silver printer"): payment scales with the customer faction's tech level and
  goodwill (QuestNode_DroidRepairJob.WealthFactor/ReputationFactor), the parts
  spent are the player's own, and minRefireDays 15 caps the frequency.

  PROVENANCE - node shapes copied from shipped defs, never recalled. Paths under
  C:\Program Files (x86)\Steam\steamapps\common\RimWorld\Data:
    GetMap, GetNearbySettlement, Delay(isQuestTimeout), Letter, End
                                    Core\...\Script_TradeRequest.xml
    GetWalkInSpot                   Core\...\Scripts_JoinerThreatCore.xml
    GeneratePawn(kindDef, faction, addToList, ensureNonNumericName)
                                    Royalty\...\Utility\Scripts_Utility_Helpers.xml
    ExtraFaction(faction, pawns, factionType HomeFaction)
                                    Royalty\...\Hospitality\Script_Hospitality_Root_Joiners.xml:322
    PawnsArrive(joinPlayer), LeaveOnCleanup
                                    Royalty\...\Script_Hospitality_Worker.xml:836,872
    ChangeFactionGoodwill(faction, change, reason)
                                    src/RimUtinni/VaultDungeons (RUT_Reclamation)
    lodgers.Arrested / .Destroyed / .LeftMap
                                    Royalty\...\Script_Hospitality_Worker.xml:1020-1160
  Royalty paths are cited for SHAPE only; every class named here lives in
  Assembly-CSharp, so this mod has no DLC dependency.

  arrivalMode is deliberately OMITTED: QuestNode_PawnsArrive defaults to
  PawnsArrivalModeDefOf.EdgeWalkIn, which is the right fiction (they walked it
  here) and is why everAcceptableInSpace is also omitted - Accept should be grey
  on a space map, because nobody walks a droid onto an orbital platform.
-->
<Defs>

  <QuestScriptDef>
    <defName>RUT_DroidRepairJob</defName>

    <!-- Route 1, the natural random pool, and it is the ONLY route: this is the
         "regular event" ruling 2 asked for. 1.1 is mid-family (shipped weights
         run 0.15-2.0).
         🔴 This def deliberately ships NO paired IncidentDef. A first draft had
         one at baseChance 0 as a deterministic test trigger; the engine refused
         it on a live 601-mod load with
             Config error in RUT_GiveQuest_DroidRepairJob:
             quest is run from both incident and random quest.
         IncidentDef.ConfigErrors() fires on `questScriptDef != null &&
         questScriptDef.rootSelectionWeight != 0f` and never looks at baseChance,
         so the two firing routes are mutually exclusive no matter how the
         incident is weighted. Force this quest with dev mode's
         Debug actions -> Quests -> Generate quest, or a jawa/fire_quest bridge
         call, both of which take a QuestScriptDef directly. -->
    <rootSelectionWeight>1.1</rootSelectionWeight>
    <rootMinPoints>0</rootMinPoints>
    <!-- Do not send a repair job to a colony that has no shop yet. -->
    <rootMinProgressScore>4</rootMinProgressScore>
    <!-- THE FREQUENCY CAP. Section 6's guardrail, enforced here. -->
    <minRefireDays>15</minRefireDays>
    <!-- The OFFER window, a different clock from the job timer below. -->
    <expireDaysRange>2~4</expireDaysRange>
    <defaultChallengeRating>1</defaultChallengeRating>

    <questNameRules>
      <rulesStrings>
        <li>questName->Repair job</li>
        <li>questName->Shop work</li>
        <li>questName->A droid at the door</li>
      </rulesStrings>
    </questNameRules>

    <questDescriptionRules>
      <rulesStrings>
        <li>questDescription->Somebody from [settlement_label] has walked a droid across the flats to your door. It is a [lodgers0_kind], it is dragging one side, and it has not run a clean cycle in a long time.\n\n[customerFaction_name] will leave it with you for [jobTicks_duration]. Fit it a part and it walks home; what you fit is your business, and theirs only when they see it move.\n\nHonest work, a standard part off your own shelf: [payHonest_money]. A part better than they are paying for: [payFine_money], and they will remember it. A worn part, and you keep the good one: [payShoddy_money], and they will remember that too.\n\nThey will not be told what you did. They will see what walks out.</li>
      </rulesStrings>
    </questDescriptionRules>

    <root Class="QuestNode_Sequence">
      <nodes>

        <li Class="QuestNode_GetMap" />
        <li Class="QuestNode_GetWalkInSpot" />

        <!-- Visitable settlements only, so the customer is always a friendly -
             ruling 2's "various local friendlies". No faction is named or
             excluded here: whoever has a settlement nearby and is not hostile
             brings work, which is exactly how a shop gets customers. -->
        <li Class="QuestNode_GetNearbySettlement">
          <storeAs>settlement</storeAs>
          <storeFactionAs>customerFaction</storeFactionAs>
          <maxTileDistance>36</maxTileDistance>
        </li>

        <li Class="QuestNode_Set">
          <name>jobTicks</name>
          <value>$(randInt(5,9)*60000)</value>
        </li>

        <!-- Unscaled defaults for the three fee figures. QuestNode_DroidRepairJob
             overwrites all three below with the wealth/reputation-scaled values;
             these exist because QuestNode.Run() SWALLOWS an exception and only
             logs it, and one unresolvable [symbol] blanks the whole description
             rather than degrading. With these set, a failure in the custom node
             costs the player accurate numbers, not the entire quest text. -->
        <li Class="QuestNode_Set">
          <name>payHonest</name>
          <value>320</value>
        </li>
        <li Class="QuestNode_Set">
          <name>payFine</name>
          <value>512</value>
        </li>
        <li Class="QuestNode_Set">
          <name>payShoddy</name>
          <value>128</value>
        </li>

        <!-- The droid. addToList, not storeAs: the object signals below are
             raised on the LIST var, and lodgers is the name vanilla uses.
             Four civilian/utility kinds, all confirmed live on the 600-mod
             capture by DROID_FACTION_LOADOUTS_1 (C1). QuestNode_RandomNode
             gives each an implicit selectionWeight of 1. -->
        <li Class="QuestNode_RandomNode">
          <nodes>
            <li Class="QuestNode_GeneratePawn">
              <kindDef>RSW_DW_KotORDroidColonist_T3UD</kindDef>
              <faction>$customerFaction</faction>
              <addToList>lodgers</addToList>
              <ensureNonNumericName>true</ensureNonNumericName>
            </li>
            <li Class="QuestNode_GeneratePawn">
              <kindDef>RSW_DW_KotORDroidColonist_R8009UD</kindDef>
              <faction>$customerFaction</faction>
              <addToList>lodgers</addToList>
              <ensureNonNumericName>true</ensureNonNumericName>
            </li>
            <li Class="QuestNode_GeneratePawn">
              <kindDef>RSW_DW_KotORDroidColonist_GE3LD</kindDef>
              <faction>$customerFaction</faction>
              <addToList>lodgers</addToList>
              <ensureNonNumericName>true</ensureNonNumericName>
            </li>
            <li Class="QuestNode_GeneratePawn">
              <kindDef>RSW_DW_KotORDroidGood_3C</kindDef>
              <faction>$customerFaction</faction>
              <addToList>lodgers</addToList>
              <ensureNonNumericName>true</ensureNonNumericName>
            </li>
          </nodes>
        </li>

        <!-- Home faction stays the customer's even while the droid sits in the
             player's faction as a lodger. This is what makes IsQuestLodger()
             true, and it is where the goodwill lands. -->
        <li Class="QuestNode_ExtraFaction">
          <faction>$customerFaction</faction>
          <pawns>$lodgers</pawns>
          <factionType>HomeFaction</factionType>
        </li>

        <!-- THE ONE CUSTOM VERB. Breaks the droid, writes payFine/payHonest/
             payShoddy to the slate, and emits the QuestPart that grades the
             work at PickupDue. Everything after this is stock. -->
        <li Class="RimMandrake.Utinni.DroidRepairJobs.QuestNode_DroidRepairJob">
          <inSignal>PickupDue</inSignal>
          <droids>$lodgers</droids>
          <customer>$customerFaction</customer>
          <basePayment>320</basePayment>
          <faultHediff>RUT_DroidJobFault</faultHediff>
          <inferiorHediffs>
            <li>RSW_DW_PartEffect_Leg_Inferior</li>
            <li>RSW_DW_PartEffect_Manipulator_Inferior</li>
            <li>RSW_DW_PartEffect_Sensor_Inferior</li>
            <li>RSW_DW_PartEffect_Motivator_Inferior</li>
            <li>RSW_DW_PartEffect_Servo_Inferior</li>
          </inferiorHediffs>
          <standardHediffs>
            <li>RSW_DW_PartEffect_Leg_Standard</li>
            <li>RSW_DW_PartEffect_Manipulator_Standard</li>
            <li>RSW_DW_PartEffect_Sensor_Standard</li>
            <li>RSW_DW_PartEffect_Motivator_Standard</li>
            <li>RSW_DW_PartEffect_Servo_Standard</li>
          </standardHediffs>
          <superiorHediffs>
            <li>RSW_DW_PartEffect_Leg_Superior</li>
            <li>RSW_DW_PartEffect_Manipulator_Superior</li>
            <li>RSW_DW_PartEffect_Sensor_Superior</li>
            <li>RSW_DW_PartEffect_Motivator_Superior</li>
            <li>RSW_DW_PartEffect_Servo_Superior</li>
          </superiorHediffs>
          <outSignalFine>WorkFine</outSignalFine>
          <outSignalHonest>WorkHonest</outSignalHonest>
          <outSignalShoddy>WorkShoddy</outSignalShoddy>
          <outSignalNeglected>WorkNeglected</outSignalNeglected>
        </li>

        <li Class="QuestNode_PawnsArrive">
          <pawns>$lodgers</pawns>
          <joinPlayer>true</joinPlayer>
          <customLetterLabel TKey="LetterLabelDroidJobArrived">Repair job: [lodgers0_nameDef]</customLetterLabel>
          <customLetterText TKey="LetterTextDroidJobArrived">[lodgers0_nameDef] came in on one good leg. [customerFaction_name] want it back inside [jobTicks_duration], moving.\n\nOpen the panel at a repair bench and fit it a part. Which part is between you and the sand.</customLetterText>
        </li>

        <!-- On cleanup rather than on a signal, so the droid leaving can never
             race the end of the quest. -->
        <li Class="QuestNode_LeaveOnCleanup">
          <pawns>$lodgers</pawns>
          <sendStandardLetter>false</sendStandardLetter>
        </li>

        <!-- The shown timer. isQuestTimeout makes it the quest's own clock, and
             PickupDue is what the grading part listens for. -->
        <li Class="QuestNode_Delay">
          <delayTicks>$jobTicks</delayTicks>
          <isQuestTimeout>true</isQuestTimeout>
          <inspectString TKey="InspectStringDroidJobTimeLeft">Pickup in: {0}</inspectString>
          <outSignalComplete>PickupDue</outSignalComplete>
        </li>

        <!-- OUTCOME 1: a superior part. Costs the player their best stock and
             buys the best name. -->
        <li Class="QuestNode_Letter">
          <inSignal>WorkFine</inSignal>
          <label TKey="LetterLabelDroidJobFine">Fine work: [lodgers0_nameDef]</label>
          <letterDef>PositiveEvent</letterDef>
          <text TKey="LetterTextDroidJobFine">[lodgers0_nameDef] walked out of your shop better than it walked in, and better than [customerFaction_name] paid for. Nobody says thank you out here. They pay, and they come back, and they tell the next settlement over.</text>
        </li>
        <li Class="QuestNode_ChangeFactionGoodwill">
          <inSignal>WorkFine</inSignal>
          <faction>$customerFaction</faction>
          <change>8</change>
          <reason>RUT_DroidJobFineWork</reason>
        </li>
        <li Class="QuestNode_End">
          <inSignal>WorkFine</inSignal>
          <outcome>Success</outcome>
          <sendStandardLetter>false</sendStandardLetter>
        </li>

        <!-- OUTCOME 2: a standard part. The honest job. -->
        <li Class="QuestNode_Letter">
          <inSignal>WorkHonest</inSignal>
          <label TKey="LetterLabelDroidJobHonest">Job done: [lodgers0_nameDef]</label>
          <letterDef>PositiveEvent</letterDef>
          <text TKey="LetterTextDroidJobHonest">[lodgers0_nameDef] cycles clean and walks straight. [customerFaction_name] paid the price you named and went home with a working droid, which is all either of you wanted.</text>
        </li>
        <li Class="QuestNode_ChangeFactionGoodwill">
          <inSignal>WorkHonest</inSignal>
          <faction>$customerFaction</faction>
          <change>4</change>
          <reason>RUT_DroidJobHonestWork</reason>
        </li>
        <li Class="QuestNode_End">
          <inSignal>WorkHonest</inSignal>
          <outcome>Success</outcome>
          <sendStandardLetter>false</sendStandardLetter>
        </li>

        <!-- OUTCOME 3: a worn part, and you kept the good one. Ruling 2's "get
             rid of problematic parts": this is the profitable, disreputable
             answer, and it is a real option, not a punishment. -->
        <li Class="QuestNode_Letter">
          <inSignal>WorkShoddy</inSignal>
          <label TKey="LetterLabelDroidJobShoddy">Paid, and watched: [lodgers0_nameDef]</label>
          <letterDef>NeutralEvent</letterDef>
          <text TKey="LetterTextDroidJobShoddy">[lodgers0_nameDef] moves. It moves the way scrap moves. [customerFaction_name] paid the short price without much said, and looked at the panel a moment longer than they needed to.\n\nThe good part is still on your shelf. That was the trade.</text>
        </li>
        <li Class="QuestNode_ChangeFactionGoodwill">
          <inSignal>WorkShoddy</inSignal>
          <faction>$customerFaction</faction>
          <change>-3</change>
          <reason>RUT_DroidJobShoddyWork</reason>
        </li>
        <li Class="QuestNode_End">
          <inSignal>WorkShoddy</inSignal>
          <outcome>Success</outcome>
          <sendStandardLetter>false</sendStandardLetter>
        </li>

        <!-- OUTCOME 4: the panel was never opened. -->
        <li Class="QuestNode_Letter">
          <inSignal>WorkNeglected</inSignal>
          <label TKey="LetterLabelDroidJobNeglected">Untouched: [lodgers0_nameDef]</label>
          <letterDef>NegativeEvent</letterDef>
          <text TKey="LetterTextDroidJobNeglected">[lodgers0_nameDef] left your shop dragging the same leg it arrived on. No fee. [customerFaction_name] will mention it to whoever asks who fixes droids around here.</text>
        </li>
        <li Class="QuestNode_ChangeFactionGoodwill">
          <inSignal>WorkNeglected</inSignal>
          <faction>$customerFaction</faction>
          <change>-8</change>
          <reason>RUT_DroidJobNeglected</reason>
        </li>
        <li Class="QuestNode_End">
          <inSignal>WorkNeglected</inSignal>
          <outcome>Fail</outcome>
          <sendStandardLetter>false</sendStandardLetter>
        </li>

        <!-- OUTCOME 5: you kept it. Ruling 2's "get rid of problematic droids",
             read from the other side: the customer's droid never leaves, and it
             is worth more than the fee ever was. Arrest is the route the game
             actually offers on a quest lodger
             (FloatMenuOptionProvider_Arrest.cs:55) - bolt it and it is yours. -->
        <li Class="QuestNode_Signal">
          <inSignal>lodgers.Arrested</inSignal>
          <node Class="QuestNode_Sequence">
            <nodes>
              <li Class="QuestNode_Letter">
                <label TKey="LetterLabelDroidJobSeized">Kept: [lodgers0_nameDef]</label>
                <letterDef>NegativeEvent</letterDef>
                <text TKey="LetterTextDroidJobSeized">[lodgers0_nameDef] is not going home. [customerFaction_name] left a droid with the Jawa and are about to learn what that has always meant.</text>
              </li>
              <li Class="QuestNode_ChangeFactionGoodwill">
                <change>-18</change>
                <faction>$customerFaction</faction>
                <reason>RUT_DroidJobDroidKept</reason>
              </li>
              <li Class="QuestNode_End">
                <outcome>Fail</outcome>
                <sendStandardLetter>false</sendStandardLetter>
              </li>
            </nodes>
          </node>
        </li>

        <!-- Stripped for parts, or it died on the bench. Vanilla uses
             .Destroyed for a dead quest pawn; .Killed has no XML precedent. -->
        <li Class="QuestNode_Signal">
          <inSignal>lodgers.Destroyed</inSignal>
          <node Class="QuestNode_Sequence">
            <nodes>
              <li Class="QuestNode_Letter">
                <label TKey="LetterLabelDroidJobDestroyed">Nothing left: [lodgers0_nameDef]</label>
                <letterDef>NegativeEvent</letterDef>
                <text TKey="LetterTextDroidJobDestroyed">There is no droid to hand back. [customerFaction_name] will be told it was beyond saving, and will decide for themselves what that means.</text>
              </li>
              <li Class="QuestNode_ChangeFactionGoodwill">
                <change>-12</change>
                <faction>$customerFaction</faction>
                <reason>RUT_DroidJobDroidKept</reason>
              </li>
              <li Class="QuestNode_End">
                <outcome>Fail</outcome>
                <sendStandardLetter>false</sendStandardLetter>
              </li>
            </nodes>
          </node>
        </li>

        <!-- Wandered off on its own: nobody's fault, no verdict. -->
        <li Class="QuestNode_End">
          <inSignal>lodgers.LeftMap</inSignal>
          <outcome>Unknown</outcome>
        </li>

        <!-- The two ways this becomes impossible through no choice of the player. -->
        <li Class="QuestNode_End">
          <inSignal>customerFaction.BecameHostileToPlayer</inSignal>
          <outcome>Fail</outcome>
        </li>
        <li Class="QuestNode_End">
          <inSignal>map.MapRemoved</inSignal>
          <outcome>Fail</outcome>
        </li>

      </nodes>
    </root>
  </QuestScriptDef>

</Defs>
